<section class="block">
<h2>Who this is for</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Startups and scale-ups</strong> in Sydney, Melbourne and Brisbane that need senior technical leadership.</li>
<li><strong>Banks, insurers and superannuation funds</strong> working to CPS 230.</li>
<li><strong>Health and research organisations</strong> building on sensitive data.</li>
<li><strong>Investors</strong> backing Australian technology companies.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>What shapes technology decisions in Australia</h2>
<p>The OAIC has published guidance on privacy for organisations using commercially available AI products and for those developing and training generative AI models, including due diligence, accuracy and care with personal information<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. For APRA-regulated entities, CPS 230 on operational risk management has applied since 1 July 2025, covering critical operations, business continuity and material service providers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Rule or standard</th><th>Who it touches</th><th>What it means for architecture</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Privacy Act and OAIC AI guidance</td><td>Organisations using or building AI with personal information</td><td>Due diligence on AI products, private deployment options and accuracy controls</td></tr><tr><td>APRA CPS 230</td><td>Banks, insurers and superannuation entities</td><td>Critical operations mapped to systems, tolerance levels, tested continuity and vendor exit plans</td></tr><tr><td>Customer security reviews</td><td>B2B software and services</td><td>Recognised controls, logging and incident response</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Related reading: <a href="/insights/ai-incident-postmortems/">AI incident postmortems</a>, <a href="/insights/slos-error-budgets-small-teams/">SLOs and error budgets</a> and <a href="/insights/private-llm-on-prem/">private LLMs on-prem</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Typical first projects</h2>
<ol>
<li>A CPS 230 technology review: critical operations, dependencies and resilience testing.</li>
<li>Private AI for staff and customers with OAIC guidance built in.</li>
<li>Cloud and platform reset with Terraform, Kubernetes and cost visibility.</li>
<li>Technical due diligence for investors.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Time zones</h2>
<p>Sydney is six to seven hours ahead of the Gulf. Australian afternoons overlap my mornings, and decisions are written up so nothing waits for the next call.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>How engagements work</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Architecture and AI readiness review</strong> (2 to 4 weeks): a clear picture of the current platform, risks and a prioritised roadmap.</li>
<li><strong>Fractional CTO</strong> (ongoing, part-time): technical strategy, architecture governance, hiring and board-level communication.</li>
<li><strong>Build and hand-off</strong>: hands-on delivery of a platform or AI capability, documented so your team can run it.</li>
<li><strong>Technical due diligence</strong>: an independent view of a target's technology for investors and acquirers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every engagement starts with a free 30-minute call. See <a href="/how-i-work/">how I work</a> for the full process.</p>
</section>
